  "notes": "C++. Qualified names keep only enclosing TYPE containers - namespaces are scoping directives, so `geo::scene::Sprite::move` is golden as `Sprite.move`, and namespace declarations are not scored definitions in any language. An out-of-line definition (`double Shape::area() const`) is a method of its class, at its own line, in its own file; the in-class declaration is a separate definition in the header. Operator overloads and destructors are definitions under their written names (`operator+`, `~Node`). One construct has no defensible single answer and is left out: `Counter::bump`'s CRTP call `static_cast<Derived*>(this)->step()`, which no resolver binds without instantiation. A method declared in-class and defined out-of-line IS answerable, and `to_line` says which of the two a call names - the out-of-line definition, chosen everywhere it comes up (`Vec2.magnitude` [52], `Describe.text` [73], `Describe` [101]).",
